[Music]  this is a call from a debt collector  please consider donating to veterans of  America we are calling you from the  investigation team of IRS do these kinds  of robo calls sound familiar how about  just plain annoying if you live in the  US the answer is probably yes  one robo calling app found that there  were 26 point three billion unwanted  robo calls made to us mobile phones in  2018 and another blocking app put that  number much higher estimating that there  were nearly 48 billion but these calls  aren't just annoying they're also  costing US citizens a lot of money  scamming them out of nine point five  billion dollars in 2017 so why can't the  FCC and Federal Trade Commission stop  them well it's not that simple robo  calls are nearly impossible to stop  here's why the Internet has made it  easier and cheaper for Robo callers in  pre-internet days phone calls were  transmitted over a landline from caller  a 2 receiver B but now through a simple  click of a button on their computer a  robo caller can call tens of thousands  of phones simultaneously on top of that  they can obscure the origin of their  calls for example a robo caller and a  country overseas can legally buy US  phone numbers and use those numbers to  make their calls their goal is to trick  recipients into thinking they're  receiving a call from the US  internet-based voice systems allows  scammers overseas to operate cheaply and  more easily to make millions of robo  calls from abroad the FCC has ordered  cell phone carriers make sure every call  that's initiated makes it through to the  receiver the point of this was in part  to prevent the carriers from neglecting  customers in rural areas where service  can be dodgy but because of these  mandates carriers are wary of blocking  suspected Robo callers out of fear of  violating these rules to help fix this  problem the FCC in 2017 adopted specific  rules that give carriers the ability to  block calls that are coming from  obviously invalid numbers for example  they can now block calls that don't  contain the right number of digits  numbers that  single digit repeated numbers that are  coming from a fake area code and more  but even with these new rules illegal  calls continue some scammers use  sophisticated tactics to hide their  identity such as spoofing for example a  call may look like it's coming from an  area code close to where the recipient  lives in order to encourage them to  answer regulators have brought hefty  fines in recent years to punish Robo  callers in an effort to regain  ill-gotten assets look at this chart it  shows the difference between how much  the FCC has ordered wrongdoers to pay  versus how much the government has  actually collected that's 208 million  dollars versus $7,000 it has to rely on  the Justice Department to collect the  fines and issues the FTC has at a  similar experience the total it's  secured in court judgments for cases  involving the violations in this chart  plus the amount requested to pay back to  wronged consumers is 1.5 billion dollars  since 2004 but so far it's only  collected 121 million dollars in May of  2018  the FCC find a florida-based company and  its top executive 120 million dollars  for making a hundred million illegal  robocalls in 2016 but agency records as  of late December indicate that no funds  had been collected one reason for the  difficulty in holding Robo callers  accountable is that they're often very  small operations that are unable to pay  the full penalties according to  enforcement officials these operations  can quickly close up shop and change  their names or hide their assets  another reason robo calls are unlikely  to disappear is that many are actually  legal and useful for the recipient this  is a courtesy call to let you know your  prescription order is ready for pickup  according to a 2018 study more than 60%  of robo calls were legitimate calls like  telemarketers reminders and alerts and  that's what's tricky there's a  difference between unwanted and illegal  you may not want to hear from a debt  collector but they may have the right to  call you because of Terms of Service  you've signed further complicating  things is that the call centers are  number sellers hired for legitimate  purposes can also be used by scammers  this makes it difficult for carriers to  distinguish between legal and illegal  robocalling  finally many Robo callers aren't  actually calling you from the US this  makes it even harder for authorities to  hold illegal robocallers accountable if  a robo caller is calling the u.s. from  another country it's difficult for  government officials to identify bad  actors and even when they are able to  identify the robo caller they may not  have the jurisdiction to punish them in  response to these problems the FCC has  demanded that the telecommunications  industry develop solutions to help stop  robo calls and spoof calling numbers the  industry has since come up with stir and  shaken to protocols that help verify  whether the phone number of a caller is  legitimate once deployed call recipients  will have a symbol pop up on their phone  that indicates whether the caller is  legitimate but even those systems won't  stop all robo calls they'll simply  signal when a call has been verified  that means your phone will still ring  when an illegal robocallers is trying to  reach you  [Music]

